<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Huang Daopo</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">c. 1245–<a href="/years/1330">1330</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/14th-century">14th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Huang Daopo was a Chinese textile artist of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yuan-dynasty">Yuan dynasty</a> who rose from poverty to become one of the best-known women of the early Chinese textile industry. Sold into marriage by her family, she ran away from Songjiang, near <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shanghai">Shanghai</a>, and sailed to Yazhou on <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hainan">Hainan</a>, where she learnt spinning and weaving from the local Li people. Around 1295 she returned to Songjiang and taught local women <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cotton">cotton</a> spinning and weaving, improving the tools for ginning, fluffing, spinning and weaving, among them a three-spindle treadle-powered machine. Her methods made her hometown a centre of textile manufacturing and had a large economic impact on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yangtze-river">Yangtze River</a> Delta.</p></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Huang was her family name; Daopo was an honorific she received later in life. She came from a poor family and ran away from home at the age of ten, unable to bear the constant ill-treatment she received after being sold into marriage. She followed the Huangpu River from Songjiang and boarded a ship for the port of Yazhou on <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hainan">Hainan</a>, where she learnt spinning and weaving from the Li people.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Works and ideas</h2><p>Back in Songjiang she taught local women and also made clothing, fine fabrics and weaving machinery, including fluffing machines and crushers. She produced mixed <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cotton">cotton</a> fabrics, coloured fabrics and fabrics with mixed warp and weft fibres. For ginning she improved a machine called the Jiaoche, so that seeds could be taken out of the cotton easily; before this, people in her hometown had peeled the cottonseed by hand, slowly and with difficulty.</p><p>In stretching, she increased the vibration of the slingshot on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cotton">cotton</a>, making it fluffier and easier to spin. She changed the structure of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/spinning-wheel">spinning wheel</a> so that the yarn snapped less often, and reworked old weaving tools, adding pick-weaving and brocading. The method she spread, known as Wunijing Technology and based on the cotton techniques of the Li, had three parts: processing, yarn spinning and weaving. It raised the efficiency and quality of cotton cloth and laid a foundation for the textile culture of the Yangtze River Delta.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Legacy</h2><p>With <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cotton">cotton</a> spinning for home weaving, women in the region could earn more, and their standing in marriage and the family rose. In folklore the sixth day of the fourth lunar month is her birthday, also called the birthday of cotton. On 20 November 1980 the Ministry of Posts and Telecommunications issued a set of four stamps honouring scientists with Huang Daopo as the fourth, the first ancient woman scientist shown on a stamp. The People's Bank of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/china">China</a> placed her on a commemorative coin in 1989.</p><p>The Huang Daopo Memorial Hall, at the Shanghai Botanical Garden in the Xuhui district, opened in 2003 with a statue of her 2.2 metres high in its courtyard. A crater on Venus is named after her. Her life has been told in a stage play directed by Gao Du of the Beijing Dance Academy and in the television series A Weaver on the Horizon.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Huang Daopo” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>By tradition the weaver <b>Huang Daopo</b>, who had fled a marriage her family sold her into, learned cotton spinning on Hainan and around 1295 taught it to the women of Songjiang.</blockquote><a href="/societies/yuan-china">Social Hierarchy of Yuan China</a></li></ul></section>
